Subject: [PATCH] reservation: base the retirement floor on other readers, exactly

txn_reserve_writes decides whether a committed occupant of a reservation slot
can be evicted. When it cannot, the slot's fingerprint stops being consulted
and any key that merely collides aborts a transaction that conflicts with
nothing. Two things about that bound were wrong, and together they made a
single connection committing in a loop abort against itself.

Which transactions the bound counts. It answers "could a live transaction
still need this record to detect a conflict of its own". The transaction
asking is not a candidate -- it has already weighed every key in its write
set against its own read version, and it is about to finish. Counting itself
only ever bars it from evicting records nothing else wants. A snapshot is
drawn one below the highest assigned sequence (txn.c), so the commit that
immediately preceded this transaction sits above its own snapshot: every slot
that commit left is permanently unretirable to the next transaction on the
same connection.

Where the bound comes from. published_min_snapshot is maintained for the
compaction gc floor, which wants to err low -- too low keeps a version a
reader might still want. This consumer wants the opposite: too low makes
occupants look unretirable and refuses commits that had no conflict. It is
also stale low by construction between compaction scans, and zero before the
first one, since tidesdb_txn_registry_publish_min_snapshot has exactly one
caller (the compaction scheduler). A database that has not compacted yet
therefore read zero on every commit, which reads as "nothing is ever
retirable". The two consumers want opposite kinds of error out of one number.

So this path now takes the exact minimum over the other live transactions.
A registry walk per commit costs about 3% of single-threaded commit
throughput, so the registry also keeps a live count, and the common case of
nobody else being joined answers from one relaxed load instead of walking
32 shards under their locks.

Measured on MySQL 9.7 with the TidesDB storage engine, 1000 sequential
single-connection transactions of 4 UPDATEs + 1 DELETE + 1 INSERT over 5000
rows with a secondary index: 2 spurious TDB_ERR_CONFLICT aborts before, 0
after, and 0 over a 5000-transaction run. No concurrency is involved, so
every one of those was a false positive.

tidesdb_bench fillrandom at isolation 3, txn_ops 6, 10s runs, 3 reps:
single threaded 373/384/378 kops/s before and 377/380/385 kops/s after;
8 threads 947/1058/1041 before and 923/1008/1008 after, a spread the
unpatched numbers alone already cover. Engine suite passes (54 binaries),
as does the MySQL plugin's MTR suite (86 tests) including its
conflict-detection cases.

diff --git a/src/txn/registry.c b/src/txn/registry.c
index 93d3e48..f1b3d17 100644
--- a/src/txn/registry.c
+++ b/src/txn/registry.c
@@ -51,11 +51,15 @@ typedef struct
  * @param shards the shard array
  * @param published_min the last minimum a scan published, for readers that cannot afford the scan
  *                      itself; starts at zero, which reads as "assume nothing"
+ * @param live the number of transactions currently joined, across every shard. kept so a caller
+ *             can tell "nobody but me is registered" from one relaxed load instead of walking
+ *             every shard under its lock to find out
  */
 struct tidesdb_txn_registry
 {
     tidesdb_txn_registry_shard_t shards[TDB_TXN_REGISTRY_SHARDS];
     _Atomic(uint64_t) published_min;
+    _Atomic(int) live;
 };
 
 /* the shard a joining transaction takes, claimed once per thread from a global counter.
@@ -135,6 +139,7 @@ int tidesdb_txn_registry_add(tidesdb_txn_registry_t *reg, tdb_txn_t *txn)
     shard->txns[shard->count] = txn;
     tdb_txn_set_registry_slot(txn, s, shard->count);
     shard->count++;
+    atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&reg->live, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
     pthread_rwlock_unlock(&shard->lock);
     return TDB_SUCCESS;
 }
@@ -157,11 +162,41 @@ void tidesdb_txn_registry_remove(tidesdb_txn_registry_t *reg, tdb_txn_t *txn)
         shard->txns[i] = moved;
         tdb_txn_set_registry_slot(moved, s, i);
         shard->count--;
+        atomic_fetch_sub_explicit(&reg->live, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
         tdb_txn_set_registry_slot(txn, -1, -1);
     }
     pthread_rwlock_unlock(&shard->lock);
 }
 
+int tidesdb_txn_registry_live(const tidesdb_txn_registry_t *reg)
+{
+    if (!reg) return 0;
+    return atomic_load_explicit(&reg->live, memory_order_relaxed);
+}
+
+uint64_t tidesdb_txn_registry_min_snapshot_excluding(tidesdb_txn_registry_t *reg,
+                                                     const tdb_txn_t *self)
+{
+    if (!reg) return UINT64_MAX;
+
+    /* same shard-at-a-time walk and the same fail-safe argument as the whole-registry form; the
+     * only difference is the one transaction left out */
+    uint64_t min = UINT64_MAX;
+    for (int s = 0; s < TDB_TXN_REGISTRY_SHARDS; s++)
+    {
+        tidesdb_txn_registry_shard_t *shard = &reg->shards[s];
+        pthread_rwlock_rdlock(&shard->lock);
+        for (int i = 0; i < shard->count; i++)
+        {
+            if (shard->txns[i] == self) continue;
+            const uint64_t snap = tdb_txn_snapshot(shard->txns[i]);
+            if (snap < min) min = snap;
+        }
+        pthread_rwlock_unlock(&shard->lock);
+    }
+    return min;
+}
+
 uint64_t tidesdb_txn_registry_min_snapshot(tidesdb_txn_registry_t *reg)
 {
     if (!reg) return UINT64_MAX;
diff --git a/src/txn/registry.h b/src/txn/registry.h
index 6885d83..0522f9f 100644
--- a/src/txn/registry.h
+++ b/src/txn/registry.h
@@ -60,6 +60,26 @@ void tidesdb_txn_registry_remove(tidesdb_txn_registry_t *reg, tdb_txn_t *txn);
  */
 uint64_t tidesdb_txn_registry_min_snapshot(tidesdb_txn_registry_t *reg);
 
+/**
+ * tidesdb_txn_registry_min_snapshot_excluding
+ * the oldest snapshot any live transaction other than self holds, or UINT64_MAX when self is the
+ * only one registered. a caller asking "may I discard something a live reader might still want"
+ * is never its own answer, so counting itself only ever bars it from what nothing else needs
+ * @param reg the registry
+ * @param self the transaction to leave out, which may be NULL or unregistered
+ * @return the oldest snapshot held by another live transaction, or UINT64_MAX when there is none
+ */
+uint64_t tidesdb_txn_registry_min_snapshot_excluding(tidesdb_txn_registry_t *reg,
+                                                     const tdb_txn_t *self);
+
+/**
+ * tidesdb_txn_registry_live
+ * how many transactions are joined right now, across every shard
+ * @param reg the registry
+ * @return the count, or 0 when reg is NULL
+ */
+int tidesdb_txn_registry_live(const tidesdb_txn_registry_t *reg);
+
 /**
  * tidesdb_txn_registry_publish_min_snapshot
  * run the scan once and publish its answer for readers that cannot afford to run it themselves.
diff --git a/src/txn/txn_commit.c b/src/txn/txn_commit.c
index 809aeb9..80e6092 100644
--- a/src/txn/txn_commit.c
+++ b/src/txn/txn_commit.c
@@ -478,11 +478,50 @@ static int txn_reserve_writes(tdb_txn_t *txn, const tidesdb_wal_entry_t *entries
     /* read once for the whole write set rather than per key. this is the bound below which a
      * committed occupant of a slot cannot still be depended on, and it is what lets the slot's
      * fingerprint tell a real same-key writer from a hash collision; without it every collision
-     * aborts a commit that had no real conflict. the published value is a scan the compaction
-     * scheduler already runs, read here as one relaxed load -- running the scan on this path would
-     * mean every registry shard, once per written key. it is only ever stale low, and low is the
-     * conservative direction */
-    const uint64_t min_snapshot = tidesdb_txn_registry_published_min_snapshot(txn->registry);
+     * aborts a commit that had no real conflict.
+     *
+     * two things about the bound have to be right or the fingerprint stops deciding anything, and
+     * a colliding key then aborts a transaction that conflicts with nothing.
+     *
+     * the first is which transactions it counts. the question the bound answers is whether some
+     * live transaction could still need this slot's record to detect a conflict of its own. the
+     * transaction asking is not a candidate: it has already weighed every key in its write set
+     * against its own read version, and it is about to finish. counting itself only ever bars it
+     * from evicting records that nothing else wants -- and on a database whose only writer is this
+     * one, it is the whole registry, so it bars everything. a snapshot is drawn one below the
+     * highest assigned sequence, so the commit immediately before this transaction began sits
+     * above its own snapshot: every slot that commit left is permanently unretirable to the next
+     * transaction on the same connection. a single connection committing in a loop then aborts as
+     * soon as one of its keys collides with one of them, with no second writer anywhere.
+     *
+     * the second is where it comes from. the published value is maintained for the compaction gc
+     * floor, which wants to err low -- too low keeps a version a reader might still want. this
+     * consumer wants the opposite: too low makes committed occupants look unretirable and refuses
+     * commits that had no conflict. a value published by the last compaction scan is stale low by
+     * construction between scans, and before the first scan it is zero, which reads as "nothing is
+     * ever retirable". the two consumers want opposite kinds of error out of the same number, so
+     * this one takes the exact minimum rather than the published one. that is a registry walk per
+     * commit -- once for the write set, not once per key -- on a path that would otherwise refuse
+     * wrongly.
+     *
+     * an empty result means no other live transaction holds a snapshot at all, and the honest
+     * bound is then this commit's own sequence: nothing below it is depended on by anyone. */
+    uint64_t min_snapshot;
+    if (tidesdb_txn_registry_live(txn->registry) <= 1)
+    {
+        /* nobody but this transaction is joined, so the walk can only answer "no other reader" --
+         * skip it and take that answer directly. a transaction joining between this load and the
+         * reservations below cannot invalidate it: it draws its snapshot from a monotonic clock,
+         * so its snapshot is at or above the sequence this commit is claiming, and a record older
+         * than that is one it reads as part of its own snapshot rather than one it needs the slot
+         * to find. this is the same fail-safe the shard-at-a-time walk already relies on */
+        min_snapshot = seq;
+    }
+    else
+    {
+        const uint64_t exact = tidesdb_txn_registry_min_snapshot_excluding(txn->registry, txn);
+        min_snapshot = (exact == UINT64_MAX) ? seq : exact;
+    }
 
     /* the intervals this batch writes are claimed first, so a point write that arrives afterwards
      * meets them. an interval cannot be claimed as a key hash -- there is no one key to hash -- so
